    <h2 id="how-it-works">Lease vs finance vs cash — the real difference</h2>
    <p>All three routes put the same car on the same driveway. What differs is <strong>what you pay for</strong> and <strong>what you are left holding</strong>:</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><strong>Lease</strong> — you pay the depreciation the car suffers while you have it, plus a rent charge set by the &ldquo;money factor&rdquo;. You finance a slice of the car, then hand it back and own nothing.</li>
      <li><strong>Finance</strong> — you pay off the whole car plus interest. Higher payment, but every dollar of principal buys equity. Our <a href="/calculators/auto-loan/">auto loan calculator</a> splits it month by month.</li>
      <li><strong>Cash</strong> — no interest, and the lowest headline cost in almost every comparison, but it commits capital that could be working elsewhere.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A car is two things at once: a service you consume and an asset you hold. Everybody consumes the same service; the decision settles whether you also want the asset. Depreciation links them and is unavoidable either way — the buyer pays it silently as a lower resale value, the lessee explicitly as a line in the payment. Our <a href="/calculators/depreciation/">depreciation calculator</a> and <a href="/depreciation/">depreciation guides</a> cover how fast body styles shed value.</p>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-title"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>The trap: comparing monthly payments</div>
      A lease almost always shows the lowest monthly payment — that's the whole appeal. But a low payment on something you'll never own is not the same as a low cost. Compare the <strong>true total cost</strong> and what you hold at the end.
    </div>

    <h2 id="lease-payment">How a lease payment is actually built</h2>
    <p>A lease payment has two components, and most advertised offers hide which is which. The split tells you what is negotiable. Here is the benchmark car line by line: $34,000, 36 months, $2,000 down, 57% residual, 6.0% lease rate, 7% sales tax.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Line on the worksheet</th><th>How it is derived</th><th class="num">Amount</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Capitalised cost</td><td>Negotiated selling price</td><td class="num">$34,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cap cost reduction</td><td>Cash due at signing</td><td class="num">&minus;$2,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Adjusted capitalised cost</td><td>$34,000 &minus; $2,000</td><td class="num">$32,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Residual value</td><td>57% of $34,000</td><td class="num">$19,380</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Depreciation to finance</td><td>$32,000 &minus; $19,380</td><td class="num">$12,620</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Monthly depreciation charge</td><td>$12,620 &divide; 36</td><td class="num">$350.56</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Money factor</td><td>6.0% &divide; 2,400</td><td class="num">0.00250</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Monthly rent charge</td><td>($32,000 + $19,380) &times; 0.00250</td><td class="num">$128.45</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Base monthly payment</td><td>$350.56 + $128.45</td><td class="num">$479.01</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sales tax on the payment</td><td>$479.01 &times; 7%</td><td class="num">$33.53</td></tr>
          <tr style="font-weight:800"><td>Monthly payment</td><td>&mdash;</td><td class="num">$512.54</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Roughly 73% of the base payment is depreciation, 27% rent charge. The depreciation portion is fixed the moment price and residual are agreed; no structuring reduces it. The only lever is the price.</p>

    <h3>Where the number 2,400 comes from</h3>
    <p>The instruction to multiply a money factor by 2,400 is repeated everywhere and explained almost nowhere. Over the term the amount outstanding falls in a straight line from the adjusted capitalised cost to the residual, so the average balance is the midpoint: ($32,000 + $19,380) &divide; 2, or $25,690. A monthly rate is the APR divided by 1,200. Multiply that by the average balance and you have the rent charge — the same as multiplying the <em>sum</em> of the two figures by the APR over 2,400. The 2,400 is 1,200 for the months, doubled to take the average.</p>
    <p>That is why the rent charge is flat: $128.45 in month one and $128.45 in month 36. A loan's interest falls as the balance amortizes; a lease's does not, because the arithmetic has already averaged it. Lease rent is not cheaper than loan interest, merely levelled. And because it is quoted as a decimal, the money factor is where markup hides most easily:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th class="num">Money factor</th><th class="num">Approx. APR</th><th class="num">Rent / mo</th><th class="num">Rent over 36 mo</th><th class="num">Payment</th><th class="num">Total lease cost</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">0.00042</td><td class="num">1.0%</td><td class="num">$21.41</td><td class="num">$771</td><td class="num">$398.00</td><td class="num">$17,418</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">0.00125</td><td class="num">3.0%</td><td class="num">$64.22</td><td class="num">$2,312</td><td class="num">$443.82</td><td class="num">$19,067</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">0.00208</td><td class="num">5.0%</td><td class="num">$107.04</td><td class="num">$3,854</td><td class="num">$489.63</td><td class="num">$20,717</td></tr>
          <tr style="font-weight:800"><td class="num">0.00250</td><td class="num">6.0%</td><td class="num">$128.45</td><td class="num">$4,624</td><td class="num">$512.54</td><td class="num">$21,541</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">0.00375</td><td class="num">9.0%</td><td class="num">$192.67</td><td class="num">$6,936</td><td class="num">$581.26</td><td class="num">$24,015</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">0.00500</td><td class="num">12.0%</td><td class="num">$256.90</td><td class="num">$9,248</td><td class="num">$649.98</td><td class="num">$26,489</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Total lease cost means $2,000 down, 36 payments, $695 acquisition and $395 disposition, no overage. The span from 0.00125 to 0.00375 — trivial-looking as a decimal — is $4,948 over three years.</p>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-title"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>Cash down on a lease is not a down payment</div>
      A capitalised cost reduction is prepaid rent, not equity. If the car is written off in month four, the insurer pays the leasing company and your $2,000 is generally gone — you hold no ownership interest to claim it against. On a purchase, a down payment survives as equity. Put as little cash into a lease as the deal allows, and check gap cover is included.
    </div>

    <h2 id="residual">The residual value is the whole game</h2>
    <p>Residual value is the price at which the lender agrees, today, to take the car back in three years. It is the largest determinant of your payment and the one number you cannot negotiate. On a subvented deal the manufacturer sets it deliberately high. Watch the same car, same price, same money factor, when only the residual moves:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th class="num">Residual</th><th class="num">Buyout price</th><th class="num">Deprec. / mo</th><th class="num">Rent / mo</th><th class="num">Payment</th><th class="num">Total 36-mo cost</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num">$17,000</td><td class="num">$416.67</td><td class="num">$122.50</td><td class="num">$576.91</td><td class="num">$23,859</td></tr>
          <tr style="font-weight:800"><td class="num">57%</td><td class="num">$19,380</td><td class="num">$350.56</td><td class="num">$128.45</td><td class="num">$512.54</td><td class="num">$21,541</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">64%</td><td class="num">$21,760</td><td class="num">$284.44</td><td class="num">$134.40</td><td class="num">$448.16</td><td class="num">$19,224</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Fourteen points of residual is $128.75 a month and $4,635 over the term. Note the rent charge moves the <em>wrong</em> way: a higher residual raises the average balance, so rent climbs $11.90 even as depreciation falls $132.22.</p>
    <p>Now the part usually left out. Our model puts this car at $19,652 after three years — $34,000 losing 20% in year one, 15% in each of the next two. Against the three buyouts:</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><strong>50%, $17,000 buyout.</strong> The car is worth $2,652 more than you can buy it for: real, capturable equity. It also signals the lender expects this model to fall hard.</li>
      <li><strong>57%, $19,380 buyout.</strong> Buyout and market value are $272 apart. Nothing to harvest either way — an honestly-set, unsubsidised residual.</li>
      <li><strong>64%, $21,760 buyout.</strong> The car is worth $2,108 <em>less</em> than the buyout. You would never exercise the option. The manufacturer has promised to absorb that $2,108 at auction and handed you the benefit as a lower payment.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>That is a subvented lease, and it is the honest answer to when leasing beats buying. Not temperament, not discipline: whether a manufacturer has chosen to buy down the residual on that model in that month. When they have, you pay less than the car's real depreciation and someone else covers the gap. When they have not, leasing is simply a dearer way to consume the same depreciation. Retention varies by body style — pickups typically hold 55&ndash;65% over five years, mainstream sedans 40&ndash;50%, EVs 30&ndash;45% — which is why lease deals cluster on some models and are absent on others. Our <a href="/calculators/new-vs-used/">new vs used calculator</a> shows the same forces from the buyer's side.</p>

    <h2 id="example">A worked example</h2>
    <p>A $34,000 car over 3 years — leased ($2,000 down, 57% residual, 6% lease rate) vs financed ($3,400 down, 7.2% APR) vs cash, at 12,000 miles a year and 7% sales tax:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th class="num">Monthly payment</th><th class="num">True 3-yr cost</th><th>At the end</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Lease</td><td class="num">~$513</td><td class="num">~$21,540</td><td>Own nothing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Finance</td><td class="num">~$948</td><td class="num">~$20,240</td><td>Car worth ~$19,650</td></tr>
          <tr style="font-weight:800"><td>Cash</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">~$16,730</td><td>Car worth ~$19,650</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The lease has by far the lowest payment ($513 vs $948) &mdash; yet it is the <strong>most expensive</strong> way to get this car over three years, because you end up with nothing.</p>
    <p>But notice how narrow the lease-versus-finance gap is: $1,299 over three years, or $36 a month, against a payment difference of $435. Financing is not dramatically cheaper as a way of consuming a car. What separates them is the $19,652 on the buyer's side of the ledger — and that only matters if the buyer keeps the car.</p>

    <h2 id="same-period">What a lease really costs over the same period</h2>
    <p>Payment-versus-payment is the wrong comparison, and so is one three-year lease against a five-year loan. Fix a period, add up every dollar that leaves your account, then ask what you hold at the end. Six years is the natural horizon: two lease cycles, one ownership cycle. Same car, benchmark terms, second lease written identically. Fuel, insurance and registration are excluded as identical on both sides.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Over six years</th><th class="num">Lease, then lease again</th><th class="num">Finance 60 mo, keep the car</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cash at signing</td><td class="num">$4,000</td><td class="num">$3,400</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Monthly payments</td><td class="num">$36,903</td><td class="num">$36,529</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Acquisition fees</td><td class="num">$1,390</td><td class="num">&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Disposition fees</td><td class="num">$790</td><td class="num">&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sales tax</td><td class="num">in the payments</td><td class="num">$2,380</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total cash out</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>$43,083</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>$42,309</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>What you hold at year six</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$12,069</td></tr>
          <tr style="font-weight:800"><td>Net six-year cost</td><td class="num">$43,083</td><td class="num">$30,240</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The outlays are within $774 of each other. Averaged over 72 months the lessee spends $598 a month, the buyer $588. On any measure of affordability these are the same path. The entire distinction, $12,843 of it, is the car in the driveway at the end. ($12,069 is the site's curve carried one year past its five-year value of $14,199.)</p>
    <p>The shape differs too. The lessee pays $512.54 for 72 months and then, if they carry on leasing, forever. The buyer pays $608.81 for 60 months and then nothing. Those twelve payment-free months are the years a serial lessee never reaches.</p>
    <p>Two caveats. The buyer takes on repair risk in years four to six once the warranty expires; our benchmark budgets $1,550 a year for maintenance, repairs and tires, and the back half carries more than its share. The lessee is under warranty throughout. Against that, the second lease assumes an identical car at an identical price in three years, which recent history suggests is optimistic.</p>
    <p>For context, the full five-year picture for this car — fuel, insurance and servicing included — is $57,631, or $0.96 a mile and $11,526 a year, close to AAA's <em>Your Driving Costs</em> 2025 average of $11,577 (at 15,000 miles rather than our 12,000). Run your own car through the <a href="/calculators/true-cost-to-own/">true cost to own calculator</a> or the <a href="/calculators/cost-per-mile/">cost per mile calculator</a> and you will see how little of that total a lease removes: it changes the depreciation and finance lines and leaves insurance, fuel and tires where they were.</p>

    <h2 id="mileage">Mileage limits and the excess-mileage charge</h2>
    <p>A lease is priced against an assumed mileage. Exceed it and you pay for the extra depreciation you caused, at a rate fixed in the contract — typically 15 to 30 cents a mile. Our benchmark uses 25 cents against a 12,000-mile allowance. The charge arrives as one bill when you return the car, which is why it surprises people.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th class="num">Actual miles / yr</th><th class="num">Excess over 36 mo</th><th class="num">At 15&cent;</th><th class="num">At 25&cent;</th><th class="num">At 30&cent;</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">12,000</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$0</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">15,000</td><td class="num">9,000</td><td class="num">$1,350</td><td class="num">$2,250</td><td class="num">$2,700</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">18,000</td><td class="num">18,000</td><td class="num">$2,700</td><td class="num">$4,500</td><td class="num">$5,400</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">20,000</td><td class="num">24,000</td><td class="num">$3,600</td><td class="num">$6,000</td><td class="num">$7,200</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Consider the middle row. AAA's average driver covers 15,000 miles a year. On a standard 12,000-mile lease at 25 cents, that ordinary driver hands over $2,250 at the end — more than the $1,299 by which financing already beat leasing. At 20,000 miles a year the bill is $6,000, some 28% on top of the whole $21,541 lease.</p>
    <p>You can buy a higher allowance at signing, normally priced below the post-hoc rate. But prepaid miles are not refunded, so it is a bet on your own driving; check three years of service records before guessing.</p>
    <p>Hence the clearest rule on this page. <strong>If you drive materially more than the allowance you can afford to buy, do not lease.</strong> High-mileage drivers consume depreciation faster than any lease prices it, and they pay twice: in the overage bill, and in never owning an asset.</p>

    <h2 id="end-of-term">Wear, disposition and early termination</h2>
    <p>Every advertised lease payment describes the middle of the contract. The costs at either end are absent from the advertisement, and that is where expected and actual cost diverge.</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><strong>The acquisition fee.</strong> Charged at signing, paid up front or rolled into the capitalised cost. Typical figures run in the $595 to $1,095 range; our benchmark uses $695. Capitalise it and you pay rent charge on it all term.</li>
      <li><strong>The disposition fee.</strong> Charged when you hand the car back, commonly $350 to $500; the benchmark uses $395. Normally waived if you buy the car or lease another from the same brand, which is precisely why it exists.</li>
      <li><strong>Excess wear.</strong> The contract defines acceptable condition and bills anything beyond it. Tires are the one to plan for: a set fitted in month 30 costs far less than the lender's charge in month 36.</li>
      <li><strong>Early termination.</strong> The expensive one. Walking away early generally means settling the remaining payments plus the gap between market value and the adjusted payoff, with no equity to offset it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The asymmetry is worth naming. A financed car can be sold at any time; the proceeds settle the loan and the remainder is yours. A lease has no such exit. That inflexibility is a real cost, and it should weigh on anyone whose job or commute might change inside three years. Our <a href="/buying-guides/">buying guides</a> cover disposal and trade-in timing.</p>

    <h2 id="cash">Paying cash, and where opportunity cost belongs</h2>
    <p>The lease-or-buy framing quietly assumes you are financing something. Cash is the third option, and on the benchmark car it is comfortably cheapest: $22,181 over five years, being $19,801 of depreciation plus $2,380 of sales tax. Financing costs $28,110 on the same basis. The $5,929 difference is precisely the loan interest.</p>
    <p>That is the arithmetic, not the decision, because $36,380 handed to a dealer is $36,380 doing nothing else. Tick the opportunity-cost box and the calculator charges the cash buyer the compounded return they gave up: $9,394 at 5% over five years, turning cash from cheapest into dearest. Set that against the $5,929 of interest and the break-even is roughly <strong>3.3% a year</strong>.</p>
    <p>We would rather explain the model's limits than let that be over-read. It charges opportunity cost on the full $34,000 held static for five years, while the financed buyer also commits $3,400 up front and repays principal monthly, none of which is credited back. The loan's average outstanding balance is about $16,468, not $30,600, so a like-for-like comparison would push the break-even well above 3.3%. If your alternative is a savings account, pay cash. If it is a mortgage at a higher rate than the auto loan, pay the mortgage down and finance the car. The <a href="/calculators/affordability/">affordability calculator</a> and <a href="/calculators/monthly-budget/">monthly budget calculator</a> handle the cash-flow side.</p>

    <h2 id="when">Who should lease, who should buy, who should pay cash</h2>
    <p>Most guides hedge at this point. We will not.</p>
    <h3>Lease if</h3>
    <p>You drive at or below the allowance and can prove it from service history; the manufacturer is subventing the residual on the model you want; you want the car for a defined period with no intention of keeping it. Test for subvention by setting the contract buyout against a realistic three-year valuation, as above. Leasing also suits anyone buying into a fast-moving technology: EV retention runs 30&ndash;45% over five years against 45&ndash;55% for a mid-size SUV, and a lease transfers that residual risk to the lender.</p>
    <h3>Finance if</h3>
    <p>You intend to keep the car past the end of the loan. That is the whole case and nearly sufficient alone — the six-year table turns on twelve payment-free months, and at ten years of ownership buying wins by a margin no lease approaches. It also applies if you drive above the allowance, or want the option to sell at any moment without penalty.</p>
    <h3>Pay cash if</h3>
    <p>The money is genuinely spare, your alternative use earns less than the loan rate after tax, and the purchase still leaves a reserve. Cash removes $5,929 of interest here. It is the right answer more often than the internet suggests, because the returns quoted against it are not risk-free and the loan rate is.</p>
    <h3>Do not lease if</h3>
    <p>You drive high mileage, your income is variable, you tend to keep cars a long time, or you are being sold on the payment rather than the price.</p>
    <p><button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-scroll="calc">Run your own numbers</button></p>

    <h2 id="business">Business use and tax treatment</h2>
    <p>For a business the answer can invert, and the treatment is genuinely different rather than merely presented differently.</p>
    <p>A leased vehicle used for business is generally an operating expense, with the business-use share of the payments deductible in the year incurred. A purchased vehicle is a capital asset, recovered through depreciation deductions over a schedule set by the tax code, sometimes with accelerated first-year allowances. Leasing tends to produce a smoother, earlier deduction; buying can produce a larger one in year one. Luxury-vehicle caps, lease inclusion amounts and business-use thresholds all bear on the outcome, and they change.</p>
    <p>None of this is advice. The rules depend on your jurisdiction, entity type, the vehicle's class, and how much of its use is genuinely business use. <strong>Speak to your accountant before letting tax treatment decide the question.</strong> Our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> sets out what our calculators do and do not model; tax deductibility is one of the things they do not.</p>

